To solve this problem, we need to determine if a given URI matches a predefined URI template and extract any variables present in the URI according to the template. The solution involves converting the URI template into a regular expression pattern that can match URIs and capture variable values. This approach ensures efficient matching and extraction using regex capabilities.

### Approach
1. **Parse the URI Template**: Convert the URI template into a regular expression by replacing variable placeholders (e.g., `{id}`) with regex capturing groups and escaping literal parts. This is done once when the class is instantiated.
2. **Match the URI**: Use the generated regex pattern to check if the input URI matches the template. If it matches, extract the captured values and map them to the variable names specified in the template.
3. **Return the Result**: If the URI matches the template, return a `MatchResult` containing the extracted variables. Otherwise, return `null`.

### Solution Code
```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.regex.*;

public final class UriTemplateMatcher {
    private final Pattern pattern;
    private final List<String> variableNames;

    public UriTemplateMatcher(String template) {
        this.variableNames = new ArrayList<>();
        this.pattern = compilePattern(template, variableNames);
    }

    private static Pattern compilePattern(String template, List<String> variableNames) {
        StringBuilder regex = new StringBuilder();
        regex.append("^");
        String[] parts = template.split("/");
        boolean firstPart = true;
        for (String part : parts) {
            if (part.isEmpty()) {
                continue; // Skip empty parts from leading/trailing slashes
            }
            if (!firstPart) {
                regex.append("/");
            } else {
                regex.append("/?"); // Allow optional leading slash
                firstPart = false;
            }
            if (part.startsWith("{") && part.endsWith("}")) {
                String varName = part.substring(1, part.length() - 1);
                variableNames.add(varName);
                regex.append("([^/]+)");
            } else {
                regex.append(Pattern.quote(part));
            }
        }
        regex.append("/?$"); // Allow optional trailing slash
        return Pattern.compile(regex.toString());
    }

    public final MatchResult match(CharSequence uri) {
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(uri);
        if (!matcher.matches()) {
            return null;
        }
        Map<String, String> variables = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < variableNames.size(); i++) {
            String varName = variableNames.get(i);
            variables.put(varName, matcher.group(i + 1)); // Groups are 1-based
        }
        return new MatchResult(variables);
    }

    // Example MatchResult implementation
    public static class MatchResult {
        private final Map<String, String> variables;

        public MatchResult(Map<String, String> variables) {
            this.variables = Collections.unmodifiableMap(variables);
        }

        public String get(String name) {
            return variables.get(name);
        }
    }
}
