2. **Convert Integer to Bytes**: Split the integer into four bytes using bitwise shifting. The integer is split in big-endian order, meaning the most significant byte is stored first.
3. **Store Bytes in Buffer**: Write the four bytes into the buffer and update the length of the byte vector to reflect the new size.

### Solution Code
```java
public ByteVector putInt(final int intValue) {
    ensureCapacity(length + 4);
    data[length++] = (byte) (intValue >> 24);
    data[length++] = (byte) (intValue >> 16);
    data[length++] = (byte) (intValue >> 8);
    data[length++] = (byte) intValue;
    return this;
}

private void ensureCapacity(final int requiredCapacity) {
